> I am not opposed to the implied world foo you bring up, I just think
> it is entirely beside the point

I haven't yet grasped your reasons. I think the talk of possible
worlds is relevant because in practise, whenever we want to use
restricted quantification to quantify over a set that is apparently
empty or potentially empty, we are talking of a nonempty set of
things existing in a nonreal world.

Things like {ro in-this-world pavyseljirna} are just not the sort of
thing we ever want to say, except in contrived examples in discussions
like these. We don't quantify over sets without entertaining the
possibility of their being nonempty.

